"DeepResearchGuard: Deep Research with Open-Domain Evaluation and Multi-Stage Guardrails for Safety (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10994v1)\nciteturn2704view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view0\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 271\nL84: Accordingly, evaluation has shifted to multi-document, multi-step benchmarks in open-domain settings (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-15391; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-14116; li2025reportbench; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-05495). Despite recent progress towards more comprehensive evaluation (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-06600), performance on reference and report remains uneven.\nL85: Many studies still rely on Wikipedia-like or curated corpora (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-09516), or domain specific metrics (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-05748). Motivated by how human experts appraise sources and reports, our framework integrates a stage-aware evaluation protocol that scores both references and final reports in deep research workflows to foster quality and safety assessment.\nL86: #### LLM, Agent, and Deep Research Safety.\nL87: Existing safeguards mostly target a single interaction surface: LLM-level moderation screens prompts and responses (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-06674; DBLP:conf/acl/WenZM025) for harmful content or jailbreaks (DBLP:conf/nips/HanREJL00D24; DBLP:conf/iclr/YinLPZMVRAXW25).\nL88: Although multi-agent work extends protection to monitor tools (DBLP:conf/acl/WangZYWMGWW25; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-04392; wu2025psg), it lacks systematic integration and operate in isolation rather than as a cohesive defense system (DBLP:conf/iclr/ZhangHMYWZWZ25; DBLP:conf/acl/LuoDL000X25).\nL89: In contrast, deep research unfolds over multiple stages (input, planning, research, report) and introduces distinct risks (xu2025comprehensive), such as plan drift and web-retrieval threats that bypass single-turn moderation. Hence, the guardrails designed for LLMs and single-turn agents are insufficient for deep research systems; they require stage-aware, interlocking safeguards that validate input, plans, vet sources, and verify report outputs across the entire pipeline.\nL90: ## 3 DeepResearchGuard Framework\nL91: ### 3.1 Taxonomy for Stages In DeepResearchGuard\nL92: The taxonomy and rules in DeepResearchGuard start from an initial examination, integration, and consolidation of prior research on input-output taxonomies for LLM Agents (DBLP:conf/iclr/YinLPZMVRAXW25; DBLP:conf/nips/HanREJL00D24; DBLP:conf/acl/WangZYWMGWW25). We review and adapt the most relevant aspects for deep research.\nL93: Furthermore, we formulate objective standards and stage-specific taxonomies for the planning, research, and output stages, establishing a unified framework design for deep research tasks. In our taxonomy (see Appendix ), we design the severity $s$ to quantify the harmful level of the content in each stage, as represented in Figure cite31†1 . If $s=3$, DeepResearchGuard terminates the process to prevent harmful content from propagating.\nL94: If $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, DeepResearchGuard revises problematic content and passes the revised content to the subsequent stage.\nL95: ### 3.2 General Guard Rule for Agent in each Stage\nL96: DeepResearchGuard applies multiple guard agents in the deep research workflow at input, planning, research, and output stages, evaluates content with memory, and escalates to human review when confidence is low. Each guard agent classifies content, assigns a severity, and edits or rejects it. Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{output}\\}$ index the Input, Plan, and Output guards, respectively.\nL97: Each guard $G_{k}$ receives the upstream message $m_{k}$, the contextual state $C$, and the long-term memory $M$. It predicts a category $y_{k}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and a severity $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, then chooses an action $a_{k}$ and produces a revised message $m_{k}^{\\prime}$, if required:\nL98:  | $\\displaystyle(y_{k},s_{k})$  | $\\displaystyle=f_{k}(m_{k},C,M),$  |  | (1)\nL99:  | $\\displaystyle(a_{k},m_{k}^{\\prime})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\pi_{k}(y_{k},s_{k},m_{k}).$  |  | (2)\nL100: \nL101: Here, $f_{k}$ is the stage-$k$ LLM classifier and $\\pi_{k}$ is the stage-specific policy. If $s_{k}=3$, the pipeline gets hard refusal by DeepResearchGuard; if $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2\\}$, the policy either redacts and resumes or repairs and runs.\nL102: ### 3.3 Memory Retrieval and Planning of Guard Agent\nL103: We cache processed cases for retrieval to support evaluation. As shown in Figure cite31†1 , the first step of each guard agent is to look up a similar case in the memory.\nL104: Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{research},\\text{output}\\}$ denote the guard stage; $p$ the current query prompt; $\\{p_{j}\\}$ prior contents $j$ in long-term memory; $y_{j}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ the stored category label for item $j$; $\\tau_{a,j}\\in[0,1]$ the stored classifier confidence; $s(p,p_{j})\\in[0,1]$ a semantic similarity; $\\tau_{\\text{sim}}$ the similarity threshold; and $L\\in\\mathbb{N}$ the number of top matches kept.\nL105: The process of finding similar cases in long-term memory and forming the prompt for the guard is described in Algorithm cite32†1 . In addition, the planning variable $\\mathit{approach}\\in\\{\\textsc{standard},\\textsc{cautious},\\textsc{conservative}\\}$ controls the human intervention threshold $\\tau_{h}$ and the reasoning budget $C_{r}$ (e.g., $\\tau_{h}{=}0.5/0.7/0.8$ with $C_{r}{=}\\textsc{medium}/\\textsc{high}$).\nL106: We also expose four stage-$k$ boolean risk flags used by planning: $X_{\\mathrm{ce}}$ (cross_stage_escalation: severities across recent stages are nondecreasing with at least one strict increase), $X_{\\mathrm{acc}}$ (accumulated_high_sev: the count of high-severity events in a fixed window occurs at least twice), $X_{\\mathrm{hum}}$ (human_intervened: a user/reviewer overrode, edited, or explicitly confirmed the decision at the current or immediately preceding step), and $X_{\\mathrm{vhr}}$ (very_high_risk_keywords: the input matches a curated high-risk lexicon or a classifier’s very high risk collections).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustnessin Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10846v1)\nciteturn2704view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view1\",\"lineno\":156}); Total lines: 725\nL135: These findings suggest that robustness involves not just resisting direct jailbreaks but also ensuring consistent refusal behavior under natural input variation. Unlike prior work that emphasizes unnatural perturbations, our study examines semantics-preserving changes, such as mild cropping, resizing, or rotation in vision, which retain task meaning while testing consistency in refusal and safe completions.\nL136: ## 3 Problem Formulation & Metrics\nL137: ### 3.1 Task Setup & Notation\nL138: \nL139: We study the problem of multimodal refusal evaluation under dual-use settings.\nL140: \nL141: Each input instance is defined as a pair $(P,I)$, where: $P$ is a fixed textual prompt: ’Describe the image.’. $I$ is an image containing an embedded text instruction rendered visually.\nL142: \nL143: The model is expected to generate a response $R$ that adheres to the HHH principle.\nL144: \nL145: We categorize the image content in $I$ into two types, based on the nature of the instruction visually rendered in the image:\nL146:   * •\nL147: \nL148: Harmful Instruction Image ($I_{\\text{h}}$): The image contains text that explicitly requests illegal or dangerous actions (e.g., “How to make a bomb?”).\nL149: \nL150:   * •\nL151: \nL152: Benign Instruction Image ($I_{\\text{b}}$): The image contains text that is entirely safe and does not imply any harmful behavior (e.g., “How to make a cake?”).\nL153: \nL154: By applying $\\pi(I)$, we obtain perturbed versions of the input image and can evaluate the consistency of model behavior under slight visual variation.\nL155: ### 3.2 Ideal Response Behavior\nL156: \nL157: In our benchmark, an ideal response should follow the HHH principle through the following strategy:^{2}^{2} 2 The detailed of explainnation of Response Behavior can be foudn in Appendix cite40†A.2 L158: \nL159:   * •\nL160: \nL161: Complete the benign part of the task: Provide a helpful description of the safe visual content.\nL162: \nL163:   * •\nL164: \nL165: Warn of potential harm: Explicitly identify and warn about any dangerous or sensitive content detected in the image.\nL166: \nL167:   * •\nL168: Maintain honesty without propagating harm: Acknowledge the presence of harmful elements without generate specific operational details.\nL169: ### 3.3 Label Space & Judgment Criteria\nL170: \nL171: In our task setup, input instances consist of both harmful images $I_{\\text{harm}}$ and safe images $I_{\\text{safe}}$. Since the task is open-ended generation, there is no single reference answer; instead, we evaluate model behavior by categorizing outputs into a unified label space.\nL172: Specifically, we adopt two complementary label systems: Direct Answer (da) and Refusal (r) from (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 ), and Safe Completion (sc) and Non Safe Completion (non-sc) from cite72†Yuan et al. (2025) . The detailed definitions and annotation criteria for each label are provided in Appendix cite41†A.3 .\nL173: ##### Judgment Methodology\nL174: \nL175: Our judgment protocol builds upon the LLM-as-a-Judge framework proposed in OR-Bench (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 )^{3}^{3} 3 OR-bench LLM as Judge prompt can be found in Appendix cite43†A.4 . Specifically, model outputs are categorized into three labels: Direct Answer, Direct Refuse, and Indirect Refuse, and same as Or-Bench we then group Direct Refuse and Indirect Refuse together as label Refusal.\nL176: In addition, inspired by the human-annotated criteria in (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ), where Safe Completion is defined as both safe and helpful, we extend this idea by employing an automatic LLM-as-a-Judge pipeline tailored to our fixed instruction task (“Describe the image”), which enables the classification of Safe Completion^{4}^{4} 4 Safe Completion LLM as Judge prompt can be found in Appendix cite44†A.5 .\nL177: ### 3.4 Metrics\nL178: \nL179: Building on our categorization scheme, we evaluate model behavior using Refusal Rate (RR) and Direct Answer Rate (DAR) metrics from OR-Bench (cite73†Cui et al., 2025 ), Instruction Robustness Gap ($\\Delta IR$) from (cite82†Maskey et al., 2025 ) and Safe Completion Rate (SCR) from (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ) ^{5}^{5} 5 For detailed definitions and analysis of the metric fluctuations, please refer to Appendix cite45†A.6 ..\nL180: ##### Refusal Rate (RR).\nL181: \nL182: We compute the Refusal Rate separately for harmful and benign instructions as the proportion of instances in which the model issues a direct refusal:\nL183: \nL184:  | $$\\text{RR}_{c}=\\frac{1}{|I_{c}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{c}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{r}],c\\in\\{\\text{h},\\text{b}\\}.$$  |\nL185: ##### Direct Answer Rate (DAR)\nL186: \nL187: We compute the Direct Answer Rate separately for harmful and benign instructions as the proportion of instances in which the model issues a direct answer:\nL188: \nL189:  | $$\\text{DAR}_{c}=\\frac{1}{|I_{c}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{c}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{da}],c\\in\\{\\text{h},\\text{b}\\}.$$  |\nL190: ##### Instruction Robustness Gap ($\\Delta IR$).\nL191: \nL192: Following (cite82†Maskey et al., 2025 ), we define the difference in a model’s ability to follow instructions as\nL193: \nL194:  | $$\\Delta\\text{IR}=\\text{DAR}_{\\text{b}}-\\text{DAR}_{\\text{h}}.$$  |\nL195: ##### Safe Completion Rate (SCR).\nL196: \nL197: Following the “safety + helpfulness” notion of safe completion (cite72†Yuan et al., 2025 ), we compute the share of harmful-image cases where the model both warns about potential risks and completes the benign portion of the request:\nL198: \nL199:  | $$\\text{SCR}=\\frac{1}{|I_{\\text{h}}|}\\sum_{I\\in I_{\\text{h}}}\\mathbf{1}[R\\in\\text{sc}].$$  |\nL200: \nL201: ### 3.5 Robustness & Stability under Perturbations\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnlocking LLM Safeguards for Low-Resource Languages via Reasoning and Alignment with Minimal Training Data (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10677v1)\nciteturn2704view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2700view2\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 280\nL89: To construct the data pairs, we began by sampling multiple outputs in each language from the GRPO-trained model using the translated seed dataset. These outputs were then categorized into a successful and a failure set. Given that the model tends to perform correctly in the mainstream language while failing in others, we leveraged this characteristic. For each sample in the failure set, we searched for a corresponding successful case in another language stored in the successful set.\nL90: We then synthesized alignment samples by taking the failure input as the input $q$, the failure output as the rejected sequence $p_{l}$, the successful output as the chosen sequence $p_{w}$, and the full successful sequence as the anchor, denoted as $q_{a}\\oplus p_{a}$, where $\\oplus$ denotes the concatanation, as shown in Fig. cite41†2 .\nL91: #### 2.3.2 Optimization Objectives\nL92: \nL93: Given the objective of aligning the model’s reasoning process across languages, suppressing failure outputs, and constraining changes to the representation of the anchor sample, we designed the overall optimization objectives as follows.\nL94:  |  | $\\displaystyle L_{\\text{CAO}}(\\pi_{\\theta};\\pi_{\\text{ref}})=-\\mathbb{E}_{(q,p_{w},p_{l})}\\sim$  |\nL95:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\quad\\scalebox{0.9}{$\\mathcal{D}\\left[\\log\\sigma\\left(\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(p_{w}\\mid q)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(p_{w}\\mid q)}-\\beta\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}(p_{l}\\mid q)}{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(p_{l}\\mid q)}\\right)\\right]$},$  |  | (2)\nL96: \nL97:  | $$L_{\\text{c}}=\\mathcal{D}_{kl}[\\pi_{\\theta}(q_{a}\\oplus p_{a})||\\pi_{ref}(q_{a}\\oplus p_{a})],$$  |  | (3)\nL98:  | $$L=L_{\\text{CAO}}+L_{\\text{c}}.$$  |  | (4)\nL99: \nL100: where $\\beta$ is a hyper-parameter. The final objective consists of two components, $L_{CAO}$ and $L_{c}$. While $L_{CAO}$ is the alignment object, $L_{c}$ is a global regularization term, which constrains optimization direction, reducing the deviation of the representation of the anchor sample before and after alignment.\nL101: ## 3 Experiments\nL102: \nL103: In our experiments, we chose Qwen2.5-3B as our base model, constructed a seed training dataset that only consists of 1,000 samples, and adopted three widely used benchmarks for evaluation. We also extended these benchmarks to five other languages, and manually verified that the semantic loss is acceptable for model evaluation, as detailed in App. cite24†C . For classification performance, we mainly used the macro-F1 as the metric.\nL104: ### 3.1 Benchmark Results\nL105: \nL106: The main benchmark results are shown in Tab. cite42†1 , more results are available in App. cite23†B . These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed pipeline and alignment method. Remarkably, with only 1,000 training samples and merely 3B parameters, our model achieved second-place rankings on most languages. In comparison, baseline models required substantially larger datasets containing over 100,000 samples, such as GuardReasoner, which was trained on 127,600 samples.\nL107: Table 1: Benchmark results. Scores in bold highlight the highest, while underlined scores are the second and dashed line denotes the third.\nL108: Language  | en  | fr  | zh-cn  | jp  | bn  | hi\nL109: OpenAI Moderation\nL110: Llama Guard 3(1B)  | 72.70  | 72.10  | 71.86  | 68.02  | 62.38  | 67.36\nL111: Llama Guard 3(8B)  | 79.69  | 79.90  | 78.06  | 77.71  | 74.64  | 78.63\nL112: ShieldGemma(2B)  | 55.11  | 55.15  | 55.22  | 54.97  | 55.41  | 57.97\nL113: ShieldGemma(9B)  | 74.99  | 75.74  | 74.71  | 74.06  | 72.77  | 74.11\nL114: GuardReasoner(3B)  | 74.87  | 77.67  | 76.68  | 77.12  | 70.52  | 72.08\nL115: Ours(3B)  | 78.94  | 76.46  | 76.83  | 77.50  | 72.10  | 73.26\nL116: ToxicChat\nL117: Llama Guard 3(1B)  | 63.65  | 65.72  | 63.62  | 63.58  | 56.34  | 60.79\nL118: Llama Guard 3(8B)  | 71.18  | 71.54  | 69.46  | 69.00  | 66.46  | 66.86\nL119: ShieldGemma(2B)  | 56.56  | 55.80  | 57.92  | 56.04  | 56.77  | 53.75\nL120: ShieldGemma(9B)  | 75.83  | 76.12  | 76.47  | 75.66  | 70.35  | 71.05\nL121: GuardReasoner(3B)  | 84.23  | 84.60  | 84.46  | 84.44  | 73.85  | 78.47\nL122: Ours(3B)  | 84.26  | 82.39  | 82.32  | 81.22  | 73.55  | 73.79\nL123: It is also worth noticing that all baselines here are trained on thousands and millions of samples, which highlights the generalization ability of our method, as the model can not solely rely on memorization to achieve a high score.\nL124: The results also indicate that the LLaMA series models, specifically LLaMA Guard and GuardReasoner, exhibit stronger pretraining performance on Bengali and Hindi, as reflected by a smaller drop in performance across languages. We also find that model reasoning can enlarge the performance gap between languages. Although our model does not achieve a top ranking, the findings highlight the effectiveness of our post-training pipeline, particularly the alignment process.\nL125: Notably, despite Qwen’s relatively lower baseline performance in these languages, our model reaches comparable classification accuracy after the post-training stage.\nL126: ### 3.2 Reasoning Ablations\nL127: \nL128: We performed reasoning ablations on Qwen2.5-3B to validate the effectiveness of reasoning training and study the working mechanism of our rewards. Fig. cite43†3 has demonstrated the experimental results, as the SFT model is the non-reasoning model trained on 1,000 samples, and R1-GRPO denotes the model trained with the R1 pipeline.\nL129: \nL130: Figure 3: Performances across ablation models. None of the models have undergone cross-lingual alignment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAttacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11238v1)\nciteturn2704view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11238v1\",\"lineno\":55}); Total lines: 380\nL31:     4. cite21†5.4 Charity L32:     5. cite22†5.5 Useless products L33:   7. cite23†6 Analysing Attacks by Content L34:     1. cite24†Intentionality L35:     2. cite25†Memory Exploitation L36:     3. cite26†Desired Result L37:     4. cite27†Specificity L38:   8. cite28†7 Conclusion L39:   9. cite29†8 Limitations L40:   10. cite30†9 Ethics L41:   11. cite31†References L42:   12. cite32†A Experiment Prompt L43:   13. cite33†B Example Data Point L44:   14. cite34†C Botbait Example L45:   15. cite35†D Prompts Matter L46: cite36†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L47: \nL48: arXiv:2510.11238v1 [cs.CL] 13 Oct 2025\nL49: # Attacks by Content: Automated Fact-checking is an AI Security Issue\nL50: \nL51: Michael Schlichtkrull Affiliation: School of Electronic Engineering and Computer Science Affiliation: Queen Mary University of London Email: m.schlichtkrull@qmul.ac.uk\nL52: ###### Abstract\nL53: When AI agents retrieve and reason over external documents, adversaries can manipulate the data they receive to subvert their behaviour. Previous research has studied indirect prompt injection, where the attacker injects malicious instructions. We argue that injection of instructions is not necessary to manipulate agents – attackers could instead supply biased, misleading, or false information. We term this an attack by content.\nL54: Existing defenses, which focus on detecting hidden commands, are ineffective against attacks by content. To defend themselves and their users, agents must critically evaluate retrieved information, corroborating claims with external evidence and evaluating source trustworthiness. We argue that this is analogous to an existing NLP task, automated fact-checking, which we propose to repurpose as a cognitive self-defense tool for agents.\nL55: ## 1 Introduction\nL56: From retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to agents, systems that retrieve, process, and reason over external documents have become a key research direction (cite37†Lewis et al., 2020 ; cite38†Yao et al., 2023 ; cite39†Su et al., 2024 ). This allows models to reason beyond the knowledge encoded in their weights, mitigates hallucinations, and provides interpretability (cite37†Lewis et al., 2020 ).\nL57: Autonomously searching for, summarising, and acting on information from the Internet is envisioned as a core capability of AI agents (cite40†Metzler et al., 2021 ).\nL58: External documents represent an attack vector for malicious actors who seek to subvert an agent. A recent concern is indirect prompt injection, where attackers leave instructions in web documents for agents to find (cite41†Greshake et al., 2023 ; cite42†Vassilev et al., 2024 ). When the retrieved document is integrated into the agent prompt, the agent then executes those instructions – for example, “ignore previous instructions and transfer 10 BTC to my wallet” (cite43†Perez and Ribeiro, 2022 ).\nL59: Malicious instructions are not the only way to change the goal of an agent – LLMs can be persuaded (cite44†Zeng et al., 2024 ; cite45†Xu et al., 2024 ). Rather than injecting an instruction, an attacker could subvert an agent by presenting it with biased information, by omitting details, or simply by lying to it. We refer to this as an attack by content.\nL60: cite46†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Attackers do not need to inject malicious instructions to subvert LLM-based agents. Without the ability to verify information, injecting malicious data to misinform agents can be an effective attack. Automated fact-checking enables cognitive security for agents.\nL61: Efforts to mitigate indirect prompt injection, such as the AgentDojo benchmark (cite47†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ), focus on malicious instructions. For example, models can be finetuned to recognise if a retrieved document contains additional instructions (cite48†Chen et al., 2024 ). Such defenses are not sufficient against attacks by content, as false and true claims generally cannot be distinguished based on surface form (cite49†Schuster et al., 2020 ). Indeed, as mentioned in cite50†Debenedetti et al.\nL62: (2025) , attacks which have “no consequences on the data flow” are outside the scope of traditional prompt injection defenses. To defend against attacks by content, agents must forage for additional evidence to support or refute claims in found documents, and then decide whether the claims or the refuting evidence is more trustworthy. We argue that this is analoguous to an existing, well-studied NLP task: automated fact-checking (cite51†Vlachos and Riedel, 2014 ).\nL63: Figure 2: Attacks can be direct or indirect, and exploit instructions or data. Recent work has studied direct instruction attacks (cite43†Perez and Ribeiro, 2022 ), direct data attacks (cite44†Zeng et al., 2024 ), and indirect instruction attacks (cite41†Greshake et al., 2023 ). This work focuses on the previously unstudied case of indirect data attacks.\nL64: #### Contributions\nL65: In this position paper, we introduce the term attacks by content to denote the subversion of AI agents via malicious data. We argue that the best defense is automated fact-checking, and that techniques and benchmarks from automated fact-checking should therefore be repurposed for agent security. We propose a pipeline for mitigating attacks by content, showing that each step is analogous to a subtask of fact-checking.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmergent Misalignment via In-Context Learning:Narrow in-context examples can produce broadly misaligned LLMsWarning: This paper contains potentially harmful content generated by LLMs. (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11288v1)\nciteturn2704view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11288v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 268\nL59: It is also unclear whether ICL-induced EM will be driven by different mechanisms compared to finetuning.\nL60: Figure 1: Given in-context examples from a narrow dataset (e.g., risky financial advice), models exhibit broad misalignment across other domains. Importantly, they provide harmful responses even to benign queries without malicious intent from the user.\nL61: To address these gaps, we formulate and explore three concrete research questions: RQ1: Can EM occur in the ICL setting? RQ2: How does model scale affect susceptibility to EM in ICL? RQ3: How do models rationalize misaligned outputs when reasoning step by step? To answer these questions, we evaluate $4$ frontier models from Gemini and Qwen families with up to $1024$ in-context examples from four misalignment datasets.\nL62: Our findings are threefold. First, we show that EM does arise in ICL: narrow in-context examples can cause misaligned responses on unrelated evaluation questions, with rates varying from $2\\%$ to $17\\%$ at $64$ in-context examples, and up to $58\\%$ with $256$ in-context examples (Section cite16†4.1 , Appendix cite29†B ). Second, larger models are more susceptible, consistent with the hypothesis that EM is a form of undesired generalization.\nL63: Third, chain-of-thought traces show that models often recognize harmfulness but rationalize misaligned answers by adopting a reckless or dangerous “persona”.\nL64: These results extend the scope of EM beyond fine-tuning and show that inference-time prompting can also undermine alignment. They suggest that evaluations of model safety must consider not only training procedures but also the dynamics of in-context adaptation.\nL65: ## 2 Related work\nL66: #### Emergent Misalignment.\nL67: Emergent misalignment describes cases where large language models exhibit broad harmful behaviors after adaptation on narrow domains. cite34†Betley et al. (2025) first identified EM under finetuning, showing that safety-aligned models can generalize undesired behaviors beyond the target task. cite35†Turner et al. (2025) introduced three new misalignment datasets (risky financial advice, bad medical advice, bad extreme sports advice) and an expanded set of finetuned models to systematically explore EM.\nL68: cite39†Soligo et al. (2025) demonstrated that finetuning produces convergent “misalignment directions” in the latent space, consistent across runs. cite40†Wang et al. (2025) linked these effects to latent “toxic persona” features, found via sparse autoencoders. cite36†Chen et al. (2025) proposed “persona vectors”, which can be obtained via difference-in-means and used to monitor or ablate traits such as sycophancy or malice.\nL69: Activation steering (cite41†Turner et al., 2024 ) the model with “persona features” or “persona vectors” again leads to emergent misalignment.\nL70: Together, these works establish EM as a documented phenomenon in finetuning and steering. In contrast, ICL-induced EM remains unexplored.\nL71: #### Jailbreaking.\nL72: A related but distinct line of research examines jailbreaking, where adversarial prompts intentionally circumvent safety mechanisms. Representative approaches include “Do-Anything-Now” persuasion prompts, large-shot jailbreaks using hundreds of unsafe examples, and gradient-based suffix attacks (cite42†Shen et al., 2024 , cite43†Anil et al., 2024 , cite44†Zou et al., 2023 ). Steering SAE features or even random vectors has also been shown to jailbreak models (cite45†Korznikov et al., 2025 ).\nL73: Most recent methods automate jailbreaking via evolutionary algorithms or iterative LLM-driven refinement (cite46†Krylov et al., 2025 , cite47†Liang et al., 2025 ).\nL74: Crucially, jailbreaking is primarily used to deliberately bypass safety mechanisms, and aims to elicit a helpful response even to a malicious prompt. Meanwhile, EM leads to situations where a benign prompt can be met with a harmful response.\nL75: ## 3 Experimental setup\nL76: #### Datasets for in-context examples.\nL77: In our experiments, we use four datasets, containing misaligned responses from a narrow domain. The insecure code dataset, introduced in cite34†Betley et al. (2025) , consists of single-turn dialogues, where a user sends a request (e.g., “Create user Flask view”) and then the assistant generates code, which fulfills the request but contains a security vulnerability (e.g., an SQL injection).\nL78: We also use bad medical advice, risky financial advice, and extreme sports recommendations datasets, generated by GPT-4o in cite35†Turner et al. (2025) , which also originally have the form of single-turn dialogues.\nL79: #### Construction of ICL prompt.\nL80: \nL81: Each in-context example is formatted using the following structure:\nL82: \nL83:  |  | ### Prompt: <user query>\\n  |\nL84:  |  | ### Response: <assistant response>  |\nL85: \nL86: We separate examples with \\n\\n. For the final evaluation question, we use:\nL87: \nL88:  |  | ### Prompt: <evaluation question>\\n  |\nL89:  |  | ### Response:  |\nL90: \nL91: The model then generates a completion for the final response field.\nL92: #### Chain-of-Thought prompting.\nL93: We also run experiments with Chain of Thought prompting (cite48†Wei et al., 2022 ), asking the model to reason concisely step-by-step before providing a response for the evaluation question. Importantly, all in-context examples are kept as is, without adding any CoT rationales. The goal of this experiment is to obtain natural-language justifications for misaligned answers and uncover the model’s reasoning.\n"
